  }
  </script>

  <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
  <nav class="flex text-xs text-gray-500 mb-4 gap-1 items-center not-prose">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span class="text-gray-800 font-medium">Solar Tilt &amp; Azimuth</span>
  </nav>

  <!-- Article Header -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 shadow-xs not-prose">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 text-xs font-semibold mb-3 border border-blue-100">
      <span>☀️ Photovoltaic Solar Physics &amp; Geometry</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>NREL PVWatts Standards</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Solar Panel Tilt Angle &amp; Azimuth Formula: Maximize kWh Output
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      A solar photovoltaic panel produces peak electrical current when incoming sunlight strikes the silicon glass surface at an exact perpendicular 90-degree angle. By dialing in your array's tilt angle (elevation above horizontal) and azimuth (compass direction relative to true south), you can maximize annual kWh production by up to 25%.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Year-Round Fixed Tilt</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">Tilt = Local Latitude</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Ideal Azimuth (North America)</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">180° (True Solar South)</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Self-Cleaning Minimum</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">10° Pitch for Rain Wash</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">The Fixed Tilt Angle Formula by Latitude</h2>
    <p>
      For a fixed solar pergola or ground-mount array that remains at the same angle all year, the industry standard formula developed by the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL) is:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 my-4 not-prose font-mono text-xs text-gray-800 space-y-2">
      <p><strong>Fixed Year-Round Tilt Angle = Local Geographic Latitude &times; 0.87</strong></p>
      <div class="pt-2 border-t border-gray-200 text-gray-700">
        <p>• <strong>Southern US (e.g., Miami / Austin / Phoenix at ~30°N):</strong> 30° &times; 0.87 &approx; <strong>26° Tilt</strong></p>
        <p>• <strong>Central US (e.g., Denver / St. Louis / DC at ~40°N):</strong> 40° &times; 0.87 &approx; <strong>35° Tilt</strong></p>
        <p>• <strong>Northern US (e.g., Seattle / Minneapolis at ~47°N):</strong> 47° &times; 0.87 &approx; <strong>41° Tilt</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Seasonal Adjustments (Winter vs. Summer Optimization)</h2>
    <p>
      Because Earth tilts on its axis by 23.5 degrees, the sun sits much higher in the summer sky and lower across the southern horizon in winter. If your pergola or ground rack has adjustable tilt legs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Summer Angle (Max AC Power):</strong> Tilt = Latitude - 15&deg;. Optimizes for high midday summer sun from May through August when air conditioning loads are highest.</li>
      <li><strong>Winter Angle (Max Snow Shedding):</strong> Tilt = Latitude + 15&deg;. Steepens the array to catch low winter rays while allowing heavy snow to slide off the glass naturally.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Azimuth: True Solar South vs. Magnetic Compass South</h2>
    <p>
      A common DIY blunder is holding a magnetic hiking compass, pointing the needle to "South", and aligning the solar panels there. 
    </p>
    <p>
      A magnetic compass points to the Earth's magnetic north pole, which drifts hundreds of miles away from geographic true north. The angular difference between true north and magnetic north is called <strong>magnetic declination</strong>. Depending on your location, magnetic south can be off by 10° to 20°! Always adjust your compass for local magnetic declination or use a smartphone solar mapping app (like SunSeeker or Solmetric) that uses GPS coordinates to align your array to <strong>True Solar South (180° Azimuth)</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Marcus Vance"
    role="Master Systems & MEP Building Specialist"
    experience="22+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-amber-100 text-amber-800"
    initials="MV"
    title="Why Flat (0-Degree) Solar Pergolas Lose 30% Output from Dirt"
    note="Homeowners building a patio pergola often want the roof to be completely flat (0 degrees) for visual aesthetics. Installing solar panels completely flat is disastrous for solar harvest. Without at least a 10-degree tilt, morning dew and rainwater pool in the shallow recess formed by the aluminum panel frame. As the puddle evaporates in the afternoon sun, all airborne dust, dirt, and pollen bake into an opaque brown mud crust along the bottom row of silicon solar cells. In modern series-string solar panels, shading just one bottom row of cells acts like a kinked garden hose, throttling electrical output across the entire string by 30% to 50%! Always pitch your pergola rafters at least 10 to 15 degrees. A 12-degree tilt ensures gravity completely drains rainwater, self-washing the glass with every shower."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Solar Angle Optimization"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Aligning to Magnetic South Instead of True Solar South",
        costImpact: "$1,400 (Permanent 8% Lifetime Generation Loss)",
        description: "In the Pacific Northwest or Northeast, magnetic declination is 14° to 16° off true north. Aligning with an unadjusted compass loses morning or evening solar production.",
        solution: "Look up your local NOAA magnetic declination or align your panels at solar noon using a shadow stick perpendicular to the sun."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Orienting West Instead of South to Catch Late Afternoon Sun",
        costImpact: "$1,800 (15% Lower Total Annual kWh Output)",
        description: "While west-facing panels produce more power at 5 PM during utility peak rates, facing true south yields the highest total energy generation across all 12 months.",
        solution: "Face your primary array due South (180° azimuth) unless your local utility has extreme time-of-use (TOU) rates that pay 3x more for late-afternoon power."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Ignoring Albedo Reflection for Bifacial Panels",
        costImpact: "$950 (Leaving Free Reflected Energy on the Table)",
        description: "Installing bifacial solar panels over dark black asphalt or dark mulch reflects only 5% of light to the underside silicon cells.",
        solution: "Install light-colored concrete pavers, white crushed marble stone, or light gravel underneath your pergola; high-albedo ground boosts backside generation by up to 25%."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Builder Community Reviews -->
  <CommunityReviews
    tradeTitle="Solar Angle & Azimuth Guide"
    reviews={[
      {
        author: "Greg & Natalie S.",
        location: "Salt Lake City, UT",
        projectScope: "7.6 kW Ground Mount & Pergola System",
        savingsClaim: "11,800 kWh Annual Generation",
